body:
`resolveInstrumentationConfig` requires each imported package to be *compilable*, but the walk only needs the package's directory and module — neither requires a build. A pure-aggregator instrumentation package (its only `.go` file carries `//go:build tools`) has empty `GoFiles`, so `packages.Load` reports `build constraints exclude all Go files` in `pkg.Errors` and `GetPackageDir` returns `""`. Both gates fire, so importing such a package **hard-fails the build** — yet that's exactly the shape the `otel/all` aggregator example in `docs/rules.md` shows.

Repro: `go list -e` on a directory whose only file is a `//go:build tools` file returns empty `GoFiles` and the build-constraint error. The PR's own `gincustom/instrumentation` avoids this only because it also has `hook.go`.

Fix: drop the `pkg.Errors` check (genuine failures are still caught by `pkg.Module == nil` above, and by `rulesFromDir`/`ErrNotInstrumentation` below), and fall back to module-relative dir derivation when `GoFiles` is empty:

````suggestion
	pkg := pkgs[0]
	if pkg.Module == nil {
		return nil, ex.Newf("package %s is not part of a module", importPath)
	}

	modDir := pkg.Module.Dir
	// Prefer the directory of a real Go file. A tools-only instrumentation
	// package (its only .go file carries //go:build tools) has no buildable Go
	// files, so packages.Load reports "build constraints exclude all Go files"
	// and GoFiles is empty; fall back to deriving the dir from the module path +
	// import-path suffix (correct even under replace directives). The walk only
	// needs the directory, not a compilable package.
	pkgDir := pkgload.GetPackageDir(pkg)
	if pkgDir == "" {
		pkgDir = filepath.Join(modDir, filepath.FromSlash(importPath[len(pkg.Module.Path):]))
	}
````

`TestResolveInstrumentationConfig` masks this today because every fixture writes a `dummy.go` — please add a fixture whose *only* file is a `//go:build tools` tool file (no `dummy.go`) plus an end-to-end `walkInstrumentation` aggregator case.

Alternatively, if pure-aggregator packages aren't meant to be supported, this becomes a docs fix: the `otel/all` example must include a build-unconstrained `.go` file (e.g. a `package all` `doc.go`) so the package loads.

